{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>You shall salt with salt.</b> It would have been sufficient to say 'and with every meal offering of yours you shall offer salt,' or 'you shall salt' — but it said במלח תמלח to explain that the salt must be of medium grade: not too coarse, so that it not draw out the blood, nor too fine, because it would dissolve in the moisture of the flesh. Now our salting of meat today is like the salting of the flesh of the offering." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the reason for salt in the offerings is that it would not be a path of honor in the offering of HaShem for it to be unsalted without salt. And the Torah teaches derech eretz, that the kingdom of heaven is like the kingdom of earth, in the manner of (Malachi 1:8): 'הקריבהו נא לפחתך' — 'present it, pray, to your governor.'" "<b>And the opinion</b> of the Rav, our Master Moses, of blessed memory, on the reason for the salt, is that the practice of idol worshippers is to keep salt far from their offerings: they despise it and do not offer it at all, because salt absorbs the blood. And they kept it far so that not even a drop of the blood would go to loss — so greatly were they drawn after theואדים and its forces. And in order to demolish their intent, the Torah commands: 'you shall not omit salt.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash there is in it an explanation that extends to the way of the Kabbalah: a covenant was cut with salt from the six days of Creation, for the lower waters were promised that they would be offered on the altar by means of salt and the water libation on the Festival. And so they said in the Midrash: the lower waters were called 'weeping waters.' And why were they called weeping waters? Because at the hour when the Holy One, blessed be He, divided the waters, setting these above and these below, the lower waters began to weep — and this is what is written (Job 28:11): 'מבכי נהרות חבש.' Rabbi Abba said: through weeping the lower waters were separated from the upper ones. They said: Woe to us, that we did not merit to ascend on high to be near our Maker. What did they do? They brazened their faces and split the depths and sought to ascend, until the Holy One, blessed be He, rebuked them, as it is said (Isaiah 43:16): 'הנותן בים דרך ובמים עזים נתיבה,' (Nahum 1:4) 'גוער בים ויבשהו.' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to them: Since you did all this for My honor, the upper waters have no permission to say song until they take permission from you, as it is said (Psalms 93:4): 'מקולות מים רבים אדירים משברי ים' — and what do they say? 'אדיר במרום ה\\''. And not only that, but you are destined to be offered upon the altar by means of salt and the water libation. And they further interpreted elsewhere: the whole world — a third of it wilderness, a third of it settled land, a third of it sea. The sea stood before the Holy One, blessed be He, and said before Him: Master of the universe, in the wilderness the Torah was given, in the settled land the Holy Temple was built — what will become of me? He said to him: Israel are destined to offer salt upon the altar." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: there are in salt two powers, opposed one to the other, and they are water and fire. By the power of the heat of the fire that dried it and congealed it, salt is restored; and if so, there is in the very substance of salt the power of water and of fire, which correspond to the two attributes through which the world is sustained, and they are the attribute of mercy and the attribute of judgment. And for this reason it says: 'ולא תשבית מלח ברית אלהיך' — it calls salt 'the covenant of your Elohim,' because through it the world is sustained and the covenant cut. And as they said: 'See that it cannot be sustained by judgment alone — associate with it the attribute of mercy.' And so too salt sustains and destroys: for it sustains and preserves flesh for a long time and gives flavor to all foods, and it destroys as well, for in a place of salt no grass at all will grow. As it is written (Jeremiah 17:6): 'ארץ מלחה ולא תשב,' and it is written (Deuteronomy 29:22): 'גפרית ומלח שרפה כל ארצה.'" "<b>And you shall not suffer the salt of the covenant of your Elohim to be lacking from your meal-offering.</b> If this refers to the meal-offering, which is fine flour, it has already been said, ״וכל קרבן מנחתך במלח תמלח״; and if it refers to the flesh-offering, it has already been said, ״על כל קרבנך״ — so why was it necessary to write, ״ולא תשבית מלח ברית אלהיך״? The sage Rabbi Abraham, of blessed memory, explained that this is an oath, like one who says: 'I adjure you by the covenant of your Elohim into which I have brought you, that you shall not suffer salt to be lacking from your meal-offering.' Up to here (his words). And it says, ״על כל קרבנך תקריב מלח״ — had it not mentioned ״על כל קרבנך״, I would have understood that He commanded to give salt only with the loaves of bread, which are a meal-offering; and this is why it mentions מנחה each time — ״קרבן מנחתך״, ״מעל מנחתך״ — for that meal-offering which is fine flour is called a קרבן מנחה, in the manner of what is written, ״ונפש כי תקריב קרבן מנחה״. But as for the other offerings that are not a meal-offering, such as sin-offering, guilt-offering, burnt-offering, and peace-offerings, I would not have understood that one must give salt with them; therefore it was necessary to say, ״על כל קרבנך תקריב מלח״ — that is, even with the other offerings of flesh you must give salt."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {2 "Reading ואדים (vapors) for the printed מאדים; the context is idolatrous rites and the forces of vapors/blood. Retained Hebrew as written without emending."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:54.760741348Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}